// CARD a5-01 · SEE: a real empty press-conference desk, the camera travelling in; the lines write across its backdrop on "June", "Donahoe" and "changed" · SEAM out right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@board_marker;pic:stock_news_press_desk_empty_01;bx:.5;by:.3;bw:.5;ink:light;dim:.42;lines:JUNE 2020^For~CEO JOHN DONAHOE^thirty-five~CONSUMER DIRECT ACCELERATION^June;src:Nike · June 2020;out:right"
For thirty-five years, that formula worked.
Then, in June twenty twenty, Nike's chief executive, John Donahoe, changed it.
He called it Consumer Direct **Acceleration**.

// CARD a5-02 · SEE: a real phone scrolling a shopping app, dim; three boxes NIKE → STORES → YOU; on "Cut" STORES is crossed out and falls, and a gold arrow draws from NIKE straight to YOU · SEAM in right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@arrow_bypass;bg:stock_payment_shopping_app_01;a:NIKE;m:STORES;b:YOU;go:plan;lab:NIKE APP · NIKE STORES;in:right"
The plan: sell through Nike's own app and stores, and cut out the **middlemen**. (J)

// CARD a5-03 · SEE: a real shopping mall crowd, dim; a list of store names; on "walked" a red line strikes them one after another · SEAM out right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@roll_call_strike;bg:stock_retail_mall_crowd_08;names:BELK~DILLARD'S~ZAPPOS~FRED MEYER~BOSCOV'S~CITY BLUE~VIM~EBLENS;go:walked;span:1.6;lab:accounts reported cut · Retail Dive, Aug 2020;out:right"
Nike walked away from Belk, Dillard's, Zappos and other **stores**.
It had already left Amazon in twenty **nineteen**.

// CARD a5-04 · SEE: the real Nike flagship in New York; a ring of shares is drawn round it; on "direct" the 44% DIRECT slice lifts · SEAM in right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@pie_on_photo;pic:nike_vs_adidas_nike_flagship_nyc;mid:44%~FY24;from:24;slices:NIKE DIRECT^44^1^#ff6b5e~WHOLESALE^56^0^#6d7380;go:By;in:right"
In fiscal twenty twenty, about three quarters of Nike brand sales still went through other people's **shops**.
By fiscal twenty twenty-four, direct sales were about forty-four **percent**.

// CARD a5-05 · SEE: CALLBACK of a0-06: the same shop shelf wall of sneakers; on "margin" two of its rows go dark and stand bare; THE SHELF · SHRINKING · SEAM out right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@shelf_empty_out;pic:nike_vs_adidas_art_store_shelf_wall;rows:.3~.42~.55;dark:.35;go:margin;word:THE SHELF · SHRINKING;out:right"
The margin was higher. But it meant the shelf was **shrinking**.

// CARD a5-06 · SEE: one real Jordan retro photo splits into 2, 4, 8 … tiles, always the same shoe · SEAM in right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@tile_multiply;tiles:nike_vs_adidas_air_jordan_4_seafoam;go:app;steps:6;from:3;to:3;count:off;hue:colours;lab:THE SAME FEW CLASSICS;in:right"
And the app kept selling the same few classics: Dunks, Air Force Ones, Jordan **retros**.
In twenty twenty-four, NPR reported the complaint that Nike had lost its innovation **mojo**.
Analysts blamed the same shoes, dropped again and again in new **colours**.

// CARD a5-07 · SEE: real sneakers left against a wall, dim; a red news band slides in on "Bloomberg" and the headline crawls into place; on "Uncool" the picture drains to grey · SEAM out right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@ticker_headline;bg:nike_vs_adidas_sneakers_yellow_wall;head:“THE MAN WHO MADE NIKE UNCOOL”;src:BLOOMBERG~SEPT 2024;go:Bloomberg;mono:Uncool;y:.3;out:right"
{wry} A Bloomberg profile of Donahoe was titled "The Man Who Made Nike **Uncool**."

// CARD a5-08 · SEE: a stack of shoe boxes beside $51.4B; on "revenue" the floor jolts, the top boxes tumble out and the figure counts down to $46.3B; −10% · SEAM in right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@box_stack_fall;bg:nike_vs_adidas_nike_hq_beaverton_wide;box:nva_cut_shoe_box;from:51.4;to:46.3;a:FY2024;b:FY2025;go:fiscal;pct:−10%;unit:$;lab:NIKE REVENUE · US$;in:right"
{grave} In fiscal twenty twenty-five, revenue fell ten **percent**.

// CARD a5-09 · SEE: a real black Nike basketball shoe as a print; on "Net" it tears and 44% of it falls away; −44% · NET INCOME · FY25 · SEAM out right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@tear_fifth;travel:.14;pic:nike_vs_adidas_nike_lebron_witness_black;go:Net;frac:.44;lab:−44%;sub:NET INCOME · FY25~$5.7B TO $3.2B;out:right"
{grave} Net income fell forty-four **percent**.
And fiscal twenty twenty-six was **flat**. (J)

// CARD a5-10 · SEE: the real Nike headquarters entrance; on "Hill" a solid red stamp INTERN · 1988 slams on; on "intern" CEO · OCT 2024 beside it; an arc: retired 2020, came back · SEAM in right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@stamp_dates;pic:nike_vs_adidas_nike_hq_entrance;y:.3;stamps:INTERN · 1988^Hill~CEO · OCT 2024^intern;gap:Elliott Hill · retired 2020 · came back;in:right"
Donahoe left. Elliott Hill came back as chief executive.
He had started at Nike as an intern in nineteen eighty-eight, and retired in twenty **twenty**.

// CARD a5-11 · SEE: MOTIF: on "job" the red shoe steps back off its plank; both lanes stand short of the shelf; NIKE OFF ITS OWN SHELF · SEAM out right
# cine act=lesson fx="code:@lane_race;bg:nike_vs_adidas_art_running_track;a:nva_cut_sneaker_red;b:nva_cut_sneaker_blue;alab:NIKE;blab:ADIDAS;ax0:0.6;ax:0.76;bx0:0.62;bx:0.62;go:plan;lab:HILL'S JOB · GET BACK ON;out:right"
His plan, Win Now, reorganised Nike around sports again and rebuilt its ties with the **stores**.
His first job: get Nike back on the **shelves**.
